{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-76356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+1,$ $y={t}^{2}-1,$ and $z={t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j+2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({t}^{2}+1)+({t}^{2}-1)-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}-t)\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+t,$ and $y={t}^{2}-t.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t+1)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4t+1+4{t}^{2}-4t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2}[({t}^{2}+t)-({t}^{2}-t)] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\sqrt{2}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2t+1}}\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(2t+1)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1,\\,t+1⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2},$ $y=t-1,$ and $z=t+1.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({t}^{2})-(t-1)^{2}-(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-({t}^{2}-2t+1)-({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}+2t-1-{t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2(2{t}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}-2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+2t)\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76377" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$    \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t+(1+t)=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1+1-0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =8t\\,i+6t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12.5"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "7.5"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-16$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-16 \\\\  & =16(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "16\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "22\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi -1"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi +1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}"] ["math" "\\pi -2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi -2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,j-2t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=3t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y={t}^{3}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =27{t}^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =28{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}28{t}^{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{4},$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}21\\cdot {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =14({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\sqrt{2}-5"] ["math" "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)"] ["math" "28\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "7(2\\sqrt{2}+1)"] ["math" "7(\\sqrt{2}-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-167612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=t+1.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{(t+1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & ={25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2} \\\\  & =125-27 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "-64"] ["math" "56"] ["math" "75"] ["math" "98"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "98"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{sin}^{2}⁡t}{5} \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4fbd84f7a7cd6147eb577ddee58cf87cb2838bc6b47ae09e6060583a07a39b03"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "q-76484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/state.json" "file_sha256" "c01cd4e9be6f51f9896edc15435cb5bc0ad8e1ae6d91c9eca68d370ada059ed4"} {"question" "e-6307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6307.json" "file_sha256" "3f6e90e94f94a8d907ddef13c574bbf642190f9031b55fd853f4ec6a34102760"} {"question" "e-6307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/assets/f53d78debfec5f8c50e0f3a5947d2a36126e18c073d7b0cc4c59d506753059b3.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $f(x,y) =  xy^3$. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t,$ and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3} \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6307.json" "file_sha256" "3f6e90e94f94a8d907ddef13c574bbf642190f9031b55fd853f4ec6a34102760"} {"question" "e-6308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6308.json" "file_sha256" "e4189d233ce250f7bb34522bc0770ffb535b8d61c77dacea51a1d0d3657092dd"} {"question" "e-6308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t.$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =xy,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{2}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20{[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6308.json" "file_sha256" "e4189d233ce250f7bb34522bc0770ffb535b8d61c77dacea51a1d0d3657092dd"} {"question" "e-6283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6283.json" "file_sha256" "59127bfc7ba150d49255026a891ac16b4098fc872c8cc6a775f18f94e88b3345"} {"question" "e-6283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x = t^2,$ $y = t,$ and $z=1.$  \n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t\\cdot 1}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u= 4t^2+1,$ $\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[{9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[27-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 26 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/example-6283.json" "file_sha256" "59127bfc7ba150d49255026a891ac16b4098fc872c8cc6a775f18f94e88b3345"} {"question" "q-40253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "02f2f5f3cd599b027cbf53f6061ffc1b66da875673dd400f0718b292116b03a6" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "Along r(t)=(t²+t,t²−t), x−y=2t, so f(r(t))=2sqrt(2)t. Also r'(t)=(2t+1,2t−1), whose magnitude is sqrt(8t²+2)=sqrt(2)sqrt(4t²+1). Their product is 4t sqrt(4t²+1), with the original limits 0 to sqrt(2). Thus original option b is correct, as confirmed by the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. The stored text key expresses the identical integral; this review preserves the captured math representation." "bindings" {"problem" "95ac3179640c49f8d7f15a781c1ba4004b00a0c7f5c8feb17da99fd5aa0030ac" "worked_solution" "2ae85fa5c835e7cbbfede2e6f99f037224c10643a69ab996d0217fbd600d7221" "original_field" "e547bd66ff276a430acf9d876793987aaea7c3e296edfe6e2c3c723fa569867f" "reviewed_field" "9a6ee7601e2b367f67decfb8f830dea7a3edac39d7f38bb74eb5926f5c04bd73" "raw_widget" "3d46ff5268b297a0c0ee0a205ea80a7681481b61ec90f9836b0ad5646b6966e6"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-40253-before.png" "sha256" "e217bc8f4a9552a55cfb6262ec963241cf56f2fac2cbea5bb5d518971e16313d"} {"path" "q-40253-before.json" "sha256" "71b8bb3851f6e5774f31b5dd14470e98e857798828c1937cbce3adfa70f8af8d"} {"path" "history-q-40253.png" "sha256" "bac2295ea358af717f223c31ac93b6cca4c4a28713085e227540f1269c713eba"} {"path" "history-q-40253.json" "sha256" "3a65fe34086e73cc6fb2f427123c2428243195aa8c449d12cc3f81290a01aeae"} {"path" "q-40253-after.json" "sha256" "59fbd7bb3d61d7193367a3c20d55128fe3f30bfb61055248892846035ce8084d"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "feb77ae2be09b98a9b8193db23b79a285681f517ddc21dc16f67eadd249e8ebf"}]} {"question" "q-40253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}t."] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t."]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053150/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "02f2f5f3cd599b027cbf53f6061ffc1b66da875673dd400f0718b292116b03a6" "rationale" "Along r(t)=(t²+t,t²−t), x−y=2t, so f(r(t))=2sqrt(2)t. Also r'(t)=(2t+1,2t−1), whose magnitude is sqrt(8t²+2)=sqrt(2)sqrt(4t²+1). Their product is 4t sqrt(4t²+1), with the original limits 0 to sqrt(2). Thus original option b is correct, as confirmed by the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. The stored text key expresses the identical integral; this review preserves the captured math representation."}] "usage" {"q-76186" [] "e-6283" [] "e-6307" [] "q-40253" [] "e-6308" []} "basis" 1774 "retractions" [[17592186134201 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}f(x, y)ds$, where $f(x, y) = \\sqrt{2}(x - y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized\n\nby $r(t) = ⟨t^{2} + t, t^{2} - t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\sqrt{2}]$?"] [17592186134201 :question/answer-fields 17592186131648] [17592186134431 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{12x}{y - 1}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t) = t^{2}i + (t + 1)j$ for $t ∈ [\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{6}]$."] [17592186133567 :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186133567 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2107/Source/Images/02b83c814a778a787ec461430e53ac92.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^3\n$$\n\n. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= f(\\cos t, \\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\cos t \\cdot (\\sin t)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\sin^{3} t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xy^{3}ds &= ∫_{a}^{b}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t\\cos t \\cdot 1dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t\\cos tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$"] [17592186133568 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1$."] [17592186133568 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nthen along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(4t)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =xy\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 3t \\cdot 4t \\\\\n&= 12t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xyds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}12t^{2} \\cdot 5dt \\\\\n&= 60∫_{0}^{1}t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 60[\\frac{t^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 20[t^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133565 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2]$."] [17592186133565 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nthen along the curve we have $x = t^2$, $y = t$, and $z=1$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j + \\frac{dz}{dt}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(1)k \\\\\n&= 2ti + 1j + 0k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dz}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{2}}{t \\cdot 1} = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4t^2+1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt &= ∫_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}u^{1/2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2} \\mid _{1}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}[9^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}[27 - 1] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 26 \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131649 17592186131650 17592186131651 17592186131652 17592186131653 17592186133029 17592186133030 17592186133031 17592186133032 17592186133033] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131648 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131648 :answer-field/id] [17592186131648 :answer-field/key] [17592186131648 :answer-field/type] [17592186133028 :answer-field/correct] [17592186133028 :answer-field/id] [17592186133028 :answer-field/key] [17592186133028 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
